## Migration Analysis

Your code has **four patterns** that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | `catch` block with rollback | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | Prevents rethrow | Remove — not needed with cooperative cancellation |
| Blocking call interruption | `GetNextItem()` may block | Pass token to unblock |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic that was in the
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop cleanly
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for the thread to finish
        if (_workerThread?.IsAlive == true)
            _workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after join, the thread is truly stuck in
        // uncooperative code. Log a warning — there is no safe
        // forced-kill equivalent in modern .NET.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

## Key changes to `GetNextItem`

The original could block indefinitely, making `Thread.Abort` the only escape. Now it **must** accept a `CancellationToken`:

```csharp
// BEFORE (.NET Framework) — blocks forever, only abortable via Thread.Abort
private WorkItem GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks until item available
}

// AFTER (.NET 8) — unblocks when token is cancelled
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException
}
```

If `GetNextItem` uses `Thread.Sleep`, replace it too:

```csharp
// BEFORE
Thread.Sleep(1000);

// AFTER — wakes immediately on cancellation
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(1000);
```

---

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing the token to `GetNextItem`** | The thread will stay stuck in the blocking call forever. There's no `Thread.Abort` to rescue it — cancellation only works if the blocking code cooperates. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and callbacks. Always `Dispose()` it. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter** | Without `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)`, you'll swallow cancellations from *other* tokens (e.g., an HTTP request timeout). |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only takes effect at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem` does long work, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` calls inside it. |
| **Using `Thread.Interrupt` as a fallback** | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9). Use `CancellationToken` throughout. |
